'NO-COMPILE
' Following a loop is not all or nothing. This body also fills k from a call the compiler may not
' make, so k is unknown once the loop ends — but n never was, and the test under the loop is still
' answered. One value it cannot work out does not cost it the ones it can.
'
' The loop tests at the bottom so that k needs no initialiser. Definite assignment runs long
' before any of this and judges a loop by its shape, so a top-tested loop guarantees nothing
' whatever the compiler later works out about it. See SPEC 7.5.
' ERROR: always TRUE
' LINE: 21
PROGRAM P
    DECLARE n INTEGER
    DECLARE limit INTEGER
    DECLARE k INTEGER
    n = 5
    limit = 7
    DO
        k = MIRROR(n)
        n = n + 1
    END DO WHILE n < limit
    IF n = 7 THEN
        ASSERT "unreachable", k >= 0
    END IF
END.
